RHI commands and command lists — implementation plan
===================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md](Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md).
Parent design task: Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.todo, Phase 8.
Status: Phases 1–3 complete; runtime migration and qualification in Phases 4–8 remain outstanding.

Goal
  Implement the approved semantic RHI boundary, immediate execution and optional single-use recorded
  lists on OpenGL 4.5+. Preserve manager-owned metadata/lifetimes, resource ownership, render order,
  binding suppression, content readiness and engine restoration while removing old execution APIs.
  The proposal controls semantics; this file controls implementation order and completion evidence.

Mandatory migration contract — applies to every implementation phase
  A migration unit includes its operations, all affected callers, blocking dependencies, creation,
  acquisition/readers, external adapters, retirement, tests and obsolete API removal. An implementation
  subtask is not a completed migration unit. Passing tests through an old forwarding path is not completion.
  No temporary compatibility facade, forwarding shim, old-name alias, duplicate dispatcher, or adapter
  whose purpose is to keep unmigrated internal callers compiling is permitted in a completed unit.
  Do not introduce such shims as intermediate implementation steps or schedule their removal for later.
  Change callers directly; keep the unit open until its dependency closure is implemented and verified.
  If a later dependency prevents that, bring it into the unit or merge the units before continuing.
  Unmigrated families may retain their original implementation, explicitly incomplete. They may call
  completed families through their final APIs; completed families must not execute through old APIs.
  Shared state remains singular throughout: migrated backend operations use the existing cache/manager
  services, never a parallel cache. The cache name changes only after its execution surface is removed.
  Permanent exact engine adapters, existing opaque resource-reference accessors and private manager
  publication handoff are intentional final boundaries, not exceptions allowing internal execution bypasses.

  Every family completion requires these receipts:
    - Before/after caller and API inventory naming removed entry points and the final routes used.
    - Source inspection showing native execution only in the family's backend modules, no shim or retry path.
    - Focused behavioral tests for effects, failure, original identity, suppression and restoration.
    - Exact external overloads preserve arguments/errors and execute/publish once without recursion.
    - Old capability variants, DSA-emulation scopes and fallback-only tests removed in that same unit.
    - Linked documentation reconciled and residual work assigned to an explicitly incomplete family.

Scope and implementation boundaries
  - Uppercase RHI in interface names/directories. OpenGlStateCache is backend-specific; existing GPU
    resource names and the implemented GpuTextureRef contract remain. No parallel resource hierarchy.
  - Managed execution uses OpenTK.Graphics.OpenGL4 and core 4.5 directly. Optional features beyond 4.5
    have focused checks; no generic fallback registry, version strategies, DSA flag or variant table.
  - Keep required bound semantics, mutable storage semantics and exact engine calls. Never retry a
    native failure through another path. Use enum members, not numeric GL tokens.
  - Preserve the approved query policy: supplied/default/derived facts; narrowly justified sample-count,
    resolved-format, shader output/interface, external acquisition/recovery and deletion-outcome reads.
    No universal GetError drain, Is-object probing, descriptor readback or requested/actual duplicate cache.
  - Immediate creation/storage/configuration/mip generation; recordable upload/copy/clear, bindings,
    complete passes, draw/dispatch, barriers, synchronization and query/readback operations.
  - No worker scheduling, asynchronous submission, shared-context execution, nested lists, cross-list
    scheduler, second backend, shader translation, render graph, global PSO cache or automatic sharing.
  - No new game launch or visual acceptance by the agent. Delegate all build/test execution to subagents.
    DEBUG-only diagnostics; no instrumented Release product. No performance baseline, benchmark,
    timing study or measured performance acceptance is required. Preserve efficient execution through
    storage reuse, binding suppression and avoidance of redundant queries or unnecessary allocations.
  - No architectural decisions remain open. Concrete API spelling, typed-page sizing and pooling are
    implementation details subject to the approved contracts, not measurement gates or scope expansion.

Phase 1: Establish final contracts and migration dependency closure
  Outcome: the final API model and explicit migration units are defined before replacing execution.
  Deliverables: [final contract definitions](Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md) and
    [source inventory, replacement units and baseline evidence](Rendering.GpuCommands.Migrations.md).
  Contracts are defined in these planning companions; production types are implemented with their
    complete families below, without empty interface scaffolding or old-executor forwarding placeholders.

  ☑ Inventory affected production callers and existing backend/native paths by operation family.
    Include wrappers/helpers, manager creation/readers/deletion, EngineStateCallMap/NativeAdapters,
    shader assets/binary caches, generated bindings, geometry, pass helpers, uniform allocators and renderers.
    Use source inspection; do not restrict supported operations to those observed in today's engine calls.
  ☑ Record each unit's old entry points, final contracts, dependencies, callers, verification and removals.
    Reconcile against existing inventory/API coverage and retained GL/Core, Ext and Arb interception scope.
    Keep legacy and other auxiliary namespaces excluded. Merge inseparable units rather than adding shims.
  ☑ Define RHICommandContext, IRHIBackend, RHICapabilities, RHICreationResult<TResource>, RHICommand
    and RHICommandList contracts, including immediate/recordable dispositions and errors/completion states.
    Define shader/PSO contracts before command payloads: RHIShaderDescription/RHIShader/RHIShaderRef,
    RHIGraphicsPipelineDescription/RHIComputePipelineDescription, RHIGraphicsPipeline/RHIComputePipeline
    and their consumer-only references with exact generation identity.
  ☑ Define project-owned descriptors/enums for initialization, ranges, geometry, attachments, pipeline
    state, bindings and synchronization. Public operations accept no GL enum, native ID, target or DSA flag.
    Keep omission versus explicit zero/false and initial-content payloads distinct from metadata.
  ☑ Retain GpuTextureRef unchanged and specify analogous typed refs only where operations require them:
    GpuBufferRef, GpuSamplerRef, GpuRenderbufferRef, GpuFramebufferRef, GpuVertexArrayRef, GpuQueryRef,
    GpuSyncRef, GpuTransformFeedbackRef, GpuSemaphoreRef and GpuMemoryObjectRef. Keep native shader,
    program and program-pipeline refs private; references grant neither deletion nor resize authority.
  ☑ Specify geometry inputs, attachment-based pass targets, execution-time uniform allocation and boundary
    declarations in the final signatures; resolve caller dependencies before writing migration code.
  ☑ Establish steady-state source ownership: Rendering/RHI/Commands, Rendering/RHI/Backends/OpenGL,
    and existing manager/metadata/initialization/wrapper domains. No aggregate dispatcher or new registry.
  ☑ Verify the contract-to-proposal mapping and unit order, especially the combined Phase 5 closure.
    Record fresh baseline build/test/package commands and evidence through delegated tools.
  ☑ Completion: contracts and dependency map cover every operation family without temporary execution
    adapters, production placeholders or an API that must be replaced again in a later phase.
  Evidence: contract/proposal second review and independent stage audit completed against the linked
    authority. Fresh delegated baseline results and exact receipt paths are in the migration companion.
    Release package/identity passed; ordinary focused Release had four disclosed observer failures,
    affected-class JIT-controlled rerun passed, and broad Debug execution was interrupted with failures.
    This establishes the starting condition, not a clean-suite or runtime-migration completion claim.

Phase 2: Establish backend admission and replace the buffer family
  Depends on Phase 1.
  Outcome: buffers execute through the final immediate RHI path, with one cache and manager authority.

  ☑ Implement backend/context association and one-time 4.5 version/entry-point admission. Project existing
    cached limits and optional capabilities through RHICapabilities; reject unsupported contexts explicitly.
    Preserve context-local tools and existing native-context identity/retirement services.
  ☑ Implement the shared operation sequence: validate/guard, suppress eligible known-equal state,
    retain original identity, withdraw affected metadata, execute explicit internal steps, reconcile and
    commit once. Disposal is cleanup, not success; no ambient collector or arbitrary joining callback.
  ☑ Implement typed owned creation using unpublished internal recipients and private manager handoff.
    Return completed owners or failure diagnostics, propagate required exceptions, publish before handoff
    and clean candidate-only allocations. Avoid constructor/factory recursion and public recipient APIs.
  ☑ Replace buffer creation/storage/configuration/upload/copy/clear and manager acquisition/deletion paths
    with direct core named operations. Preserve wrapper growth policy and GpuBufferUploader responsibility.
    Initial CPU content is stable until return; GPU staging follows explicit completion lifetimes.
  ☑ Update all affected helpers and callers directly, including uniform/geometry storage callers where
    they consume buffer operations. Remove old buffer execution entry points and try-named/retry fallback.
    The uniform publication algorithm and geometry draw family remain explicitly incomplete until Phase 5.
  ☑ Move this family's exact engine overloads to internal backend forwarding modules with compiler-checked
    signatures, reserved-name semantics and shared reconciliation. Keep metadata readers manager-owned.
  ☑ Test content, capacity, omitted inputs, partial creation failure, local contexts, same-ID reuse and
    queued retirement. Prove uploads do not publish capacity, native work never retries, and sentinel
    bindings survive named operations and necessary external bound operations.
  ☑ Completion: mandatory migration receipts pass for the entire buffer unit; no old buffer executor
    remains behind the new API. Other resource families are identified as incomplete, not shimmed.

  Evidence: second review and independent stage audit completed against all linked authority.
    Source closure, removed APIs, delegated tests and final package identities are recorded in
    [Buffer migration qualification](Rendering.GpuCommands.Migrations.md#buffer-migration-qualification-phase-2).
    Debug scoped 673 passed, final affected rechecks 53/84 passed, generator 180 passed. Ordinary Release
    675: 671 passed and four Harmony observer/injection failures; the same Release binaries passed all
    13 affected cases with process-only JIT controls. Canonical package and 761 shader identities passed.
    No instrumented Release variant, performance measurement or installed-game qualification is claimed.

Phase 3: Replace images, samplers and backing-resource relationships
  Depends on Phase 2.
  Outcome: owned and borrowed image resources use final creation, mutation and lifetime routes.

  ☑ Implement texture/renderbuffer/sampler creation, storage/configuration, uploads/copies/clears and
    immediate mip generation. Use core Create* and named APIs where equivalent; keep mutable image
    definitions bound with backend-owned restoration, never substitute immutable storage semantics.
  ☑ Migrate DynamicTexture2D, DynamicTextureCube and other image wrappers/helpers without removing
    allocation-policy inputs. Include cube faces/mips, multisample images and cached format/sample limits.
    Publish coherent image facts once, with only the approved narrow driver-derived reads.
  ☑ Implement explicit buffer-texture and native texture-view creation with backing refs and subresource
    ranges. Preserve dependencies, alias validity and view-relative selections through replacement/failure.
  ☑ Migrate external borrowing/adoption and native readers. Reuse available manager facts; unknown external
    facts use explicit acquisition. Native IDs stay at integration boundaries; ownership transfer is explicit.
    Owner refs follow intentional replacement; old borrowers/views retain original associations.
  ☑ Migrate image/sampler disposal and exact engine operations together with their caller/helper coverage.
    Failure clears provisional identity, retires only owned candidates and preserves documented replacement
    behavior; no deletion of borrowed backing objects and no invented rollback.
  ☑ Implement buffer-to-texture/texture-to-buffer transfer payload contracts with offsets/layout/pitches
    so later compute consumers need no raw pixel-buffer scopes or pointer-shaped semantic APIs.
  ☑ Test cube/mip derivation, partial allocation cleanup, contents, view ranges, replacement/name reuse,
    borrowed lifetime, sampler configuration, optional features and selector preservation. Remove baseline
    capability forks and fallback tests from wrappers, creation, readers and fixtures in this unit.
  ☑ Completion: image/sampler family receipts pass with no old executor. Framebuffer pass realization
    remains in Phase 5 and must call these completed image operations through their final contracts.

  Evidence: second review and independent stage audit completed against the linked authority.
    [Image migration qualification](Rendering.GpuCommands.Migrations.md#image-migration-qualification-phase-3)
    records source closure, direct caller migration, exact adapters and final identities. Debug 929 and
    generator 180 passed. Ordinary Release: 917 passed, 12 Harmony observer/injection failures; the same
    Release binaries passed all 929 with process-only JIT controls. Canonical package and all 761 shader
    identities passed. No instrumented Release product, live visual or performance qualification claimed.

Phase 4: Replace synchronization, queries and remaining non-draw resource operations
  Depends on Phase 3.
  Outcome: final immediate completion and auxiliary-resource operations are ready for render submission.

  ☐ Implement semantic access barriers, fence insertion, GPU waits and explicit immediate CPU polling/waits.
    Preserve ordering and existing retirement; do not infer hazards/render graphs or fence every operation.
  ☐ Implement query creation, timestamp/statistics operations and owned-staging readback requests with
    Pending/Ready/Failed/Cancelled outcomes. Mapping requires readiness; CPU dispatch is not GPU completion.
    Retain staging/mapped ownership through actual completion, including errors and context retirement.
  ☐ Implement supported semaphore/memory-object creation/import/configuration and synchronization with
    explicit optional feature checks and handle ownership. Reject unsupported requests before dispatch.
  ☐ Replace transform-feedback resource creation/configuration and other remaining non-draw operations
    identified by Phase 1. Coordinate execution-dependent work with Phase 5; merge a unit if dependency
    inspection shows its caller closure requires draw execution. Do not declare a partial family complete.
  ☐ Move corresponding exact adapters, acquisition, helpers and deletion paths into their final backend
    modules. Preserve kind-specific deletion outcomes and original-context queue admission.
  ☐ Test ready/pending/failure/cancellation, staging reuse, explicit waits, context loss and unsupported
    optional requests. Mark unavailable hardware cases as evidence gaps, not successful execution.
  ☐ Completion: each accepted unit meets mandatory receipts; all Phase 5 synchronization/transfer
    dependencies use final operations with no temporary bridge to old StateCache execution methods.

Phase 5: Replace shader, pipeline and immediate rendering as one integration unit
  Depends on Phases 2–4 and Phase 1's final shader/PSO contracts.
  Outcome: RHICommandContext is the sole immediate graphics/compute submission context.
  This is one completion gate with ordered work packages below. Shader/PSO internals may be developed
  first, but the unit cannot complete while old program/pipeline/context consumers require shims.

  ☐ Implement immutable stage-code/entry-point/specialization/binding descriptions and owned RHIShader.
    Keep asset loading/preprocessing/permutations/offline and binary-cache policy above native execution.
    Backend privately compiles/specializes/links or installs binaries; native resources stay manager-owned.
    Require optional SPIR-V support or supplied supported GLSL; no silent translation or asset substitution.
  ☐ Implement CreateShader and BeginCreateShader with pending completion, diagnostics, cancellation,
    original associations and candidate-only cleanup. Clean temporary stages; expose no partial executable.
    Install fixed ready generations; external executable changes invalidate use without in-place RHI relink.
  ☐ Implement graphics/compute PSO creation and refs, target/layout/stage validation, immutable state and
    dynamic-state declarations. Equality includes behavior and exact shader generation, not labels.
    Keep renderer-owned reuse; no global cache, reference-counted sharing, duplicate linking/reflection
    or invented native registry entries for purely managed PSOs. PSO disposal does not dispose its shader.
  ☐ Migrate reload installation across shader owners and renderer PSOs together: build compatible sets,
    swap between submissions, retain installed sets on failure, reject stale candidates and retired refs.
    Retained live old generations remain usable; prepared tables cannot silently retarget new interfaces.
  ☐ Implement geometry inputs and backend VAO realization/configuration using core named operations.
    Move upload/adoption, manager validation, vertex/index/indirect binding, draws and retirement together.
    Replace GraphicsGeometry.Submit, EngineFullscreenGeometry and CloudSimpleGeometry execution routes;
    adapters expose inputs, not native draws. Preserve ownership and callback-scoped engine borrowing.
    Validate descriptors/alignment/known bounds; producers own index and GPU-generated argument values.
    No index/indirect content readback or GPU synchronization merely to validate those contents.
  ☐ Implement attachment-based descriptions and context/backend-scoped framebuffer realization/reuse.
    Key by original associations, subresources and native configuration/routing, not bare IDs or wrapper
    equality; load/store/clear values do not create new realizations. Explicit surfaces use integration rules.
    Manager owns metadata/dependencies; check completeness before pass effects, clean failed candidates,
    invalidate dependent cache entries on replacement/deletion and retire owned targets on context teardown.
    Preserve borrowed engine framebuffer ownership and existing pass guards/restoration helpers.
  ☐ Migrate generated bindings, ShaderPreparedSubmission, GpuPreparedBindings, CpuUniformBuffer,
    UniformPublication and allocators together. Preparation uses supplied metadata; full sets validate before
    binding. Logical snapshots carry bytes/revision/lifetime; physical storage is selected in the current
    execution epoch. Successful binding commits only the captured version, never a newer CPU edit.
    Preserve persistent/transient storage completion rules, candidate abort and compatibility history.
  ☐ Implement final resource binding, dynamic state, pass sequencing, direct/indexed/instanced/indirect
    draw and compute dispatch on RHICommandContext. Reuse helper responsibilities; no duplicate state.
    Preserve stable-binding suppression, image level/layer/access/format and alias feedback validation.
  ☐ Implement boundary admission using declared pipeline sets and state footprints. Capture selectively
    before mutation; reject undeclared operations before dispatch. Restore on success/failure and retain
    existing usage/reentry guards. Compute and graphics share the context; renderers only orchestrate.
  ☐ Reconcile separately scheduled PSO multi-bind work with the current checkout: use core 4.5 multi-bind
    and existing slot knowledge, with singles for semantic eligibility/batching policy, not old contexts.
    Do not reimplement separately completed work or retain its older-context fallback after migration.
  ☐ Migrate all affected renderers, shader owners and external integration callers directly. In particular,
    preserve direct-lighting geometry, depth hierarchy dispatch→barriers→tail transfers→publication,
    cloud borrowing/content certificates, screen resize and reload/disposal order. Resource validity is
    not content freshness; producer admission remains outside the RHI and must survive these migrations.
  ☐ Move this unit's exact engine overloads/readers/retirement and remove superseded shader/PSO APIs,
    GraphicsCommandContext, adapter-owned draws and raw render dispatch. No temporary program/PSO facade.
  ☐ Test synchronous/pending/binary shader creation, failures/cancel, immutable identity and atomic reload;
    PSO ownership/reuse; known-bound geometry checks/expired borrows; target cache reuse/subresources/
    replacement/name reuse/incomplete cleanup; uniform epochs/new edits/failures; pass feedback and
    selective restoration; complete graphics and compute output parity and publication counts.
  ☐ Completion: every work package and mandatory family receipt passes; all callers use final immediate
    contracts, the old graphics context is deleted, and no old execution path is required for compilation
    or runtime. Split acceptance is prohibited even if isolated shader or pipeline tests already pass.

Phase 6: Implement single-use command recording on the final immediate machinery
  Depends on Phase 5.
  Outcome: lists record context-independent inputs and execute through the same backend as immediate calls.

  ☐ Implement immutable RHICommand headers, reusable typed payload pages and list-owned reference tables.
    Copy spans/arrays/mutable descriptions, retain immutable executable descriptions, and avoid closures,
    arbitrary callbacks, unmanaged managed-reference unions or mandatory per-command heap allocation.
  ☐ Implement exclusive list ownership and Recording→Closed→Executing→Executed/Failed lifecycle,
    cancellation before execution, reset only after terminal state, and no replay/concurrent cancellation.
    Closing freezes/publishes complete arguments for ownership transfer; context identity is explicit.
  ☐ Add recordable upload/copy/clear, bindings/dynamic state, full passes, draw/dispatch, barriers,
    synchronization, query and readback operations. Keep general creation/storage/mips immediate.
    Query resources preexist recording; fence markers return pending tickets, not provisional GpuSyncRefs.
  ☐ Record only supplied shape/balanced-pass checks. No native context, mutable resource inspection,
    manager/cache access, pass guards, target realization, uniform allocation or history publication.
    Build closed-list boundary declarations from immutable descriptions and operation kinds.
  ☐ Execute on the originating context thread in explicit order; validate limits/optional features and
    original/current resource identity as appropriate before affected work. Owner-following texture refs
    resolve at execution; shader/PSO refs and table identity stay fixed. Recheck bounds and feedback.
    Existing boundaries must admit the list footprint; no silent widening or implicit state isolation.
  ☐ Execute captured uniform snapshots through Phase 5 publication services in the current epoch.
    Cancelled lists allocate nothing and never clear dirty work; recorded equality cannot suppress an
    upload merely because an earlier unexecuted recording promised it.
  ☐ Implement failure index/original error, stop without retry/rollback, unwind scopes and withdraw only
    uncertain knowledge. Fail/cancel unexecuted requests; retain completion ownership for enqueued work.
    Context retirement cancels CPU work without GL into a replacement context. Reset releases CPU storage,
    not GPU-used staging; successful Submit adds neither Finish nor an automatic fence.
  ☐ Exercise representative graphics, depth and borrowed-resource paths with recording where their existing
    usage interval permits; keep immediate default and producer freshness checks. Lists cannot straddle
    engine callbacks or leave passes active for another list. Do not extend an engine borrow for convenience.
  ☐ Test no-current-context recording, frozen arrays/descriptions, close/reset/cancel, expired refs/borrows,
    replacement/reload, optional rejection, partial failure, pending fence cancellation, readback staging,
    execution-time uniform allocation and immediate/recorded output/state equivalence.
  ☐ Completion: all recorded operations use final backend execution without a second dispatcher authority,
    metadata path or compatibility facade; lifecycle and failure receipts cover every result-bearing path.

Phase 7: Finish the cache split and reconcile architecture documentation
  Depends on Phase 6; prior family cleanup is a prerequisite, not work deferred into this phase.
  Outcome: final naming/layout matches the approved ownership boundaries throughout the repository.

  ☐ Audit every Phase 1 family against its receipts and all remaining native dispatch sites. A surviving
    old executor/shim reopens its owning unit; do not relabel it an allowed integration path.
  ☐ Rename the remaining StateCache to OpenGlStateCache with all callers updated in the same change.
    Leave no old-name forwarding type/alias. Confirm it owns knowledge/suppression/restoration facts and
    effect forwarding, not a public native executor. Manager reads/creation invoke backend services without
    recursive creation; native query services remain manager-owned.
  ☐ Confirm exact engine adapters are the permanent compiler-checked boundary, only allowed namespaces
    are covered, original overload/error semantics survive, and native backend calls cannot recurse.
  ☐ Reconcile Rendering.GpuResources.md, Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md and affected architecture/API
    documents with implemented state. Update active inventory/coverage and parent plan cross-references;
    do not turn permanent documentation into migration history or add external-engine comparisons.
  ☐ Completion: final source audit finds no internal execution bypass, duplicate authority, temporary shim,
    obsolete capability path or old context/cache alias. Documentation describes implemented contracts.

Phase 8: Qualify integrated behavior and release artifacts
  Depends on Phase 7.
  Outcome: correctness, package identity and outstanding live evidence are explicit.

  ☐ Run delegated focused and integrated regressions on an immutable candidate, covering all family receipts,
    original-context retirement, aliases/views, local tools, context loss, rejected commands, exactly-once
    external calls and immediate/recorded equivalence. Keep hardware coverage gaps explicit.
  ☐ Run canonical Debug/Release build and package tooling through subagents; verify installed/package
    identity where the repository workflow requires it. Confirm diagnostic counters remain DEBUG-only.
    Do not create an instrumented Release product.
  ☐ Review storage reuse and allocation sites; verify immediate calls need no recording allocation and
    recording requires no per-command heap object. Use focused behavioral checks for stable binding
    suppression and reuse of available metadata without redundant queries. No timing thresholds or
    before/after benchmark comparison are required for completion.
  ☐ Provide a user-run visual/runtime checklist for graphics, compute, cloud/depth content ordering,
    resize, shader reload and teardown. Record actual user evidence separately from headless tests;
    leave live appearance/runtime-behavior acceptance unchecked until supplied.
  ☐ Reconcile every proposal requirement with implementation and evidence; resolve deviations or obtain
    explicit scope approval rather than declaring unimplemented requirements complete.
  ☐ Completion: all required correctness/package receipts and user-run acceptance are recorded;
    remaining unsupported hardware evidence is disclosed, and no unapproved architecture deviation remains.

Final acceptance
  ☐ All eight phases and mandatory migration receipts are satisfied; later cleanup never substitutes for
    removing superseded APIs within their owning migration unit.
  ☐ Final APIs are backend-neutral, managed OpenGL execution requires 4.5, and every native operation has
    one execution owner with existing manager metadata/lifetime authority and preserved cache behavior.
  ☐ GraphicsCommandContext and StateCache's old execution/name surfaces are gone; shader/PSO, geometry,
    targets, uniforms, synchronization and recording follow the approved final contracts.
  ☐ Tests, current-state docs, package verification and user-run evidence support the claimed outcome
    without performance claims or treating headless results as visual acceptance.

Proposal coverage map
  Boundary, operations, capabilities and resource inputs: Phases 1–5; final cache split in Phase 7.
  Creation/adoption, initial content, original identity and retirement: Phases 2–5; lifecycle tests in 6/8.
  Shader/PSO creation, generation identity, replacement and ownership: Phase 5; recorded rejection in 6.
  Geometry, attachment targets and immediate submission: Phase 5; recorded consumption in 6.
  Uniform capture, physical publication and history: Phase 5; recording/epoch/cancellation tests in 6.
  Recording, ownership transfer, tickets, failure and CPU/GPU completion: Phases 4/6.
  Native dispatch, publication, exact integration and no-shim migration: mandatory receipts, Phases 2–7.
  Representative outcomes, efficient-execution checks, package and user-run qualification: Phase 8, supported
    by focused verification and current-state documentation in every owning migration unit.
